WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:04.649
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:04.649 --> 00:00:09.470
Today, we’re talking about a show you’ve
all been requesting for a really long time.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.550
Our favorite scifi nightmare -- Black Mirror.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.910
Black Mirror tackles a variety of issues posed
by technology, and while it may not have a

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.860
consistent narrative between episodes, we’ve
noticed one theme that keeps coming back.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:27.730
Whether the show is tackling virtual reality,
politics, social media, or interspecies erotica,

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:29.860
Black Mirror really loves spectacle.

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.620
If watching Black Mirror gives you a sinking
feeling of dread, or disgust, it may be because

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:41.270
this idea of spectacle isn’t science fiction,
but is built into the fabric of our reality.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.780
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy
of Black Mirror.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.969
And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:48.910
So what is spectacle?

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:53.540
It’s not just fireworks, pro wrestling or
your drunk uncle at Thanksgiving, it’s really

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:55.980
any kind of compelling visual display.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:02.350
TV, video games, the internet, real life - all
these things are essentially spectacles.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.500
But if there’s one kind of spectacle that
pervades our daily reality, it’s the kind

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.310
that comes through our screens.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.100
If you haven’t noticed, Black Mirror is
obsessed with screens.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.759
The show’s promotional art features a cracked
screen, and, you know, the title is a reference

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:17.920
to this.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.920
The relationship between screen and viewer
is central throughout the show.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.920
In The National Anthem, an on-screen spectacle
mesmerizes a nation, while a performance artist

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.520
releases a hostage in broad daylight.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.000
In Nosedive, smartphone screens -- and, uh,
eye screens?

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.079
-- Compel everyone to rate their fellow humans just
like one might rate a hipster bar on

00:01:38.079 --> 00:01:39.079
Yelp.

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:47.569
In The Waldo Moment a screen literally becomes
a politician, and some of the show’s first

00:01:47.569 --> 00:01:51.810
moments visually emphasize the screens surrounding
the British cabinet.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:56.219
We can better understand Black Mirror’s
screen fixation through the lens of philosopher

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.300
Guy Debord.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.020
In the 1960s, Debord suggested that understanding
“spectacle” was critical to understand

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:03.720
society.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:08.020
In the aptly named “Society of the Spectacle,
Debord wrote that in the decades following

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:12.720
the industrial revolution, images and appearances
had begun to govern the world.

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:16.780
Many of the protagonists in Black Mirror experience
spectacle in their own way.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:19.870
Bing is immersed by screens in Fifteen Million
Merits.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:24.260
White Bear turns the justice system into a
literal spectacle of justice.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:25.520
"Murderer!"

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:30.400
In the National Anthem, the merits of pig
sex are mostly discussed in terms of public

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.400
perception.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.660
However, one of 
the most illustrative examples is Nosedive,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:46.599
where a young woman named Lacie, like many
of us, derives her self-worth from her smartphone

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:50.130
- speaking of which, please don’t forget to thumbs
up this video.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:55.329
According to Debord, "The spectacle is not
a series of images, but a social relationship

00:02:55.329 --> 00:02:58.640
between people that is mediated by images".

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.920
To over simplify: we’ve been numbed by years
of advertising and mass culture and, because

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.780
of that, live shallow, disconnected lives.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:11.840
In Nosedive, What we’re going to call “yelp
for people” has reengineered society into

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:19.640
a new class system based on popularity, which
is in turn, based on spectacular social media

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.920
profiles and fake kindness.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.920
Lacie constantly does things she otherwise
wouldn’t: she takes photos of food she finds

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:34.780
disgusting and is overly nice to people who
play an accessory role in her life;

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:36.700
"Hope you're having a great night."

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.100
"It's pretty good."

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.800
all in order to get positive rating on an app which
attributes “objective value" to people.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.600
Nay Nay is putting on a ridiculous show whenever
she opens up a device.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:57.020
Lacie’s coworker has to be unnaturally kind
and beg for positive ratings.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.140
"They're from the organic stall at the farmer's market."

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.580
Nobody is free to be their authentic selves
because, as Lacie says: "

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.760
"That's how the f***ing world works!"

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.700
People develop relations with their phones
and apps, which in turn develop relationships

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:11.769
with other humans.

00:04:11.769 --> 00:04:17.549
In other words, to recap Debord, our relationships
are mediated by images, but in this case,

00:04:17.549 --> 00:04:19.530
images delivered by our phones.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:25.140
For Debord, the more we recognize our own
existence in the terms set forth by the spectacle,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.139
the less we understand our “own existence”
and our “own desires.”

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.620
What Lacie wants is simple: a fancy new house.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.360
"We need you around a 4.5"

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.900
"4.5"

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:43.360
For that, she needs to boost her social media
score and make good with Nay Nay, her high

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.699
school friend who had sex with Lacie’s boyfriend.

00:04:46.699 --> 00:04:51.920
In true Debordian fashion, Lacie’s increasing
immersion into spectacle, makes her a hollow

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:57.140
shell of her former self before finally going
cray cray at Nay Nay’s wedding.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.940
Lacie fails in both being herself and being
someone she’s not.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:07.600
"I'm so honored to be here to see this sh*t. I love you Nay Nay!"

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.960
The spectacle has not only defined her desire,
but made her a prisoner to it.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.680
It’s only when Lacie is forcefully removed
from spectacle and thrown in jail that she

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.860
seems to find authenticity.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:19.820
"F*ck you!"

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:21.380
But how did we get here?

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:26.259
How did the society of spectacle take over
the lives of Lacie, Bing, Prime Minister Callow,

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.639
and even our own lives?

00:05:27.639 --> 00:05:30.680
Debord provides an answer that resonates in
Black Mirror.

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:35.639
Debord argues that spectacle is born when
the economy invades the very fabric of our

00:05:35.639 --> 00:05:36.889
social lives.

00:05:36.889 --> 00:05:41.889
In Fifteen Million Merits, we see what this
economic invasion looks like as entertainment

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:44.860
dictates every aspect of social life.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:50.099
Bing pointlessly rides a bike to accumulate
virtual currency he can only spend on necessities,

00:05:50.099 --> 00:05:52.710
entertainment, or his, uh, Mii?.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:57.349
He and everyone else lives in a cube made
with screens, where he constantly receives

00:05:57.349 --> 00:05:59.860
pop up ads he has to ignore.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:03.449
Things that exist outside of this mii-conomy
are frowned upon.

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:08.759
In one telling scene, paper origami is discarded
as garbage because it doesn’t exist in the

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.880
circuit of labor and commodities offered by
what I'm going to call...

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:15.500
George Orwell's favorite soul-cycle class.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:22.340
"I love choosing my own clothes and I love gold. I feel like it really expresses who I really am."

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:27.430
In an attempt to escape the spectacle, Bing
becomes obsessed with authenticity and decides

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:31.530
to do something meaningful for someone else,
namely getting a woman he heard singing in

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:33.520
the bathroom a shot at becoming famous.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:44.020
But some Simon Cowell wannabe has other plans.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:07:00.900
The economic incentive to pimp out Abi outweighs
whatever hopes and dreams she, or Bing, had.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:05.160
Bing even explicitly reflects on the way that
the economy has infiltrated their lives:

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:17.920
but, as we learn, even dissent becomes a commodity.

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:35.560
Bing gets a lavish apartment, everyone remains
miserable and the spectacle goes on, literally.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:40.930
This economic invasion of everyday life, for
Debord, happens at a very specific moment:

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:46.240
when “being” becomes “having,” when
who you are is defined by what you have.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:51.880
This logic is everywhere in our own society:
wearing a Dead Kennedys shirt makes you a rebel,

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:57.530
guzzling pepsi makes you an activist and having
a bumper sticker makes you whatever kind of

00:07:57.530 --> 00:07:59.340
pretentious you aspire to be.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:02.900
Black Mirror follows this logic, but takes
it a step further.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:07.900
In Be Right Back, a grieving woman purchases
a life-sized avatar of her boyfriend after

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.600
he dies.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:11.520
"I won't bite."

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.150
“Having” a relationship replaces “being”
in a relationship.

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:20.560
The shard of glass Bing used to threaten to
kill himself becomes a virtual commodity - “owning”

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.520
rebellion replaces “being” rebellion.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:31.830
The Waldo Moment further explores what happens
when this logic enters the political sphere.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:37.190
It follows Gwendolyn Harris, an aspiring politician,
and Jamie Salter, a comedian who voices a

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.830
foul-mouthed cartoon bear that becomes a global
icon.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.460
The Waldo Moment focuses on the unreality
of politics.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:49.500
Gwendolyn truly wants to be a politician,
but struggles with the spectacle of it all.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:51.920
While she seems genuinely concerned with policy,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:57.380
"I was at least attempting to represent s- well I don't know, not just bullocks to everything."

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.520
her campaign is just a show for her future aspirations.

00:09:00.520 --> 00:09:03.820
"The way you describe it, it's like you're doing this for a show reel."

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:04.320
"No!"

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.000
"So it's good exposure. You're not gonna win, you know you're not gonna win."

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.240
Meanwhile, Jamie is a depressed joke-making
nihilist who begrundgingly runs for office.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.080
Except, people seem way too into his message.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:17.240
Waldo is pure spectacle,

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:20.300
"There's no point in attempting to converse with a cartoon."

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:27.320
"Oooh, converse, your lordship, thy flowery language doth give me a right throbbing bone-on."

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:32.000
his only metric for
success seems to be attention.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:39.640
"...hey, hey, please Mr. Monroe. Hey, hey, good morning everybody! Why're you ignoring me, Mr. Monroe?

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:45.860
Why're you ignoring me, Mr. Monroe? Mr. Monroe, why're you ignoring me?

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.080
And, like any spectacle, it’s pure image
with no substance.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.280
Jamie constantly reminds us Waldo isn’t
real but as it turns out, that doesn’t seem

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:55.080
to really matter.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.440
"Waldo's not real."

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:01.500
"Exactly, that's what you said that really hit home. He's not real, but he's realer than all the others.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.880
Spectacle is bigger than any of its individual
actors, an autonomous system - like if Skynet

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.880
decided to only care about cat videos and reality
tv.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:14.200
And, for the “real” politicians like Monroe,
who have always succeeded via spectacle,

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.520
"I think we should have a round of applause for the children. It is fantastic."

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:20.920
they become powerless in the face of a much better
spectacle.

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:27.160
"A career politician, someone else less real than me and I can do this!"

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:33.880
Monroe’s last-minute attempts to appeal
to “real” politics are pointless, because

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:36.410
the spectacle had won long ago.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:41.380
For Debord, the system gives us competing
spectacles that are nothing more than “vaporous

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:45.500
qualities meant to stimulate loyalty to quantitative
trivialities.”

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:50.600
In other words, just as spectacle compels
Yankees fans to hate Red sox fans, it compels

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:54.010
people to identify with a blue bear who makes
fart jokes.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:11:00.440
The system can profit as much from the Yankees
vs Red Sox as it can from Waldo vs the status quo.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.980
"Everyone's pissed with the status quo and Waldo gives that a voice."

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:05.080
"Waldo's a bear."

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:07.540
As with Bing, dissent is a commodity.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:08.960
"You could roll this out worldwide."

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:09.840
"Like Pringles."

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:10.600
"Absolutely."

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:13.000
Or, as Debord says

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:27.080
So here’s a question: are we heading towards
our inevitable destruction like Black Mirror

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.150
imagines?

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:29.150
Well, maybe there's hope.

00:11:29.150 --> 00:11:35.450
The show balances its pessimism with San Junipero
in which two women fall in love in a simulated

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:39.500
reality and choose to live there for eternity
instead of accepting death.

00:11:39.500 --> 00:11:45.280
In this world, the spectacle of San Junipero isn’t a nightmare, but literally heaven.

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.930
Debord wrote The Society of the Spectacle
in the sixties, so he was mostly talking about

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.310
the world dominated by television, radio,
movies and mass-advertisement.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:59.060
He didn’t see or predict the advent of smartphones
or social media, but he certainly foresaw

00:11:59.060 --> 00:12:02.200
how they would shape society in a pretty prophetic
way.

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:03.830
What do you think Wisecrack?

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:07.400
Will spectacle drive us to the hellish world
of Black Mirror?

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.240
Or are we already there?

